{"name":"Electric Slide","hex":"#9db0b9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electric","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, produced by, operated with, or utilising electricity; electrical.","Of or relating to an electronic version of a musical instrument that has an acoustic equivalent."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electricity; the electricity supply.","An electric powered version of something that was originally or is more commonly not electric.\n An electric car, locomotive, train etc.\n\nAn electric toothbrush.\n\nAn electric typewriter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electric"},{"term":"slide","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To (cause to) move in continuous contact with a surface.","To move on a low-friction surface."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An item of play equipment that children can climb up and then slide down again.","A surface of ice, snow, butter, etc. on which someone can slide for amusement or as a practical joke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/slide"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electric Slide","blocks":[{"text":"The Electric is a four-wall line dance. Choreographer and dancer Richard L. \"Ric\" Silver claims to have created the dance in 1976."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_Slide"},{"title":"Electric Slide (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Electric Slide is a 2014 American biographical crime film written and directed by Tristan Patterson. It stars Jim Sturgess as Los Angeles–based bank robber Eddie Dodson, who robbed 64 banks in 1983 before he was caught. It premiered at the 2014 Tribeca Film Festival and received a limited theatrical release in April 2015."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/37/Electric_Slide_poster_cannes_small.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_Slide_(film)"},{"title":"Electricity","blocks":[{"text":"Electricity is the set of physical phenomena associated with the presence and motion of matter possessing an electric charge. Electricity is related to magnetism, both being part of the phenomenon of electromagnetism, as described by Maxwell's equations. Common phenomena are related to electricity, including lightning, static electricity, electric heating, electric discharges and many others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg/330px-London_MMB_%C2%BB1E6_Lightning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Thales.jpg/330px-Thales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Thales.jpg/330px-Thales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A fictional portrait of Thales, the earliest known researcher into electricity","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThales.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/500px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/1280px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg/500px-Franklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Benjamin Franklin conducted extensive research on electricity in the 18th century, as documented by Joseph Priestley (1767) History and Present Status of Electricity, with whom Franklin carried on extended correspondence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFranklin-Benjamin-LOC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg/500px-M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg/500px-M_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Michael Faraday's discoveries formed the foundation of electric motor technology.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM_Faraday_Th_Phillips_oil_1842.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg/500px-Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg/500px-Lichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An electric arc provides an energetic demonstration of electric current.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALichtbogen_3000_Volt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Panasonic-oxyride.jpg/500px-Panasonic-oxyride.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Panasonic-oxyride.jpg/500px-Panasonic-oxyride.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pair of AA cells. The + sign indicates the polarity of the potential difference between the battery terminals.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APanasonic-oxyride.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Electric_motor_cycle_3.png/500px-Electric_motor_cycle_3.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm